                    <tr><td class="metric-name">Products powered</td><td class="metric-value">Adobe Firefly, Photoshop, and all facial-feature AI tools</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="qualitative">
                <p>Adobe adopted the fairness standards and data quality protocols from this program across their full AI data pipeline. The dataset we built &mdash; and the integrity controls we put around it &mdash; became the baseline for all subsequent annotation work, including video. The client's satisfaction wasn't just with the images. It was with the framework.</p>
            </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-section">
            <div class="cs-section-label">Relevance</div>
            <div class="relevance-box">
                <div class="relevance-item">Demonstrates end-to-end ownership of a Responsible AI data initiative &mdash; from bias detection in production through systematic gap analysis, structured collection, and measurable remediation &mdash; with real stakes and real outcomes.</div>
                <div class="relevance-item">Shows the operational reality of ethical AI: data provenance is a legal requirement, not a principle. Enforcing consent, IP verification, and authenticity controls in remote regions with no digital infrastructure is a program management problem, not just an ethics one.</div>
                <div class="relevance-item">Illustrates how fairness auditing expands scope when done rigorously &mdash; the program grew 5&times; not because the contract required it, but because the data demanded it. That kind of proactive gap identification and structured remediation is what separates a responsible AI program from a checkbox exercise.</div>
            </div>
        </section>
